{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why\nCompaction preserves folded events as `*.retired` (I1, epic b306) permanently, so tombstones + the whole per-event commit/tree history accumulate on `tickets`. This blocks health-lane bug `defensive-buckskin-rhino` (`a453-193b-2864-451b`): the blobless CI checkout pack is ~481 MiB vs a deliberate 100 MiB budget (`verify-identity.yml:47`). `git gc`/repack does NOT shrink it (proven). The only lever is reducing reachable commit/tree objects on `tickets` history — causally-safe history reclamation, which needs the causal-stability proof this epic owns.\n\n## Operator decision (2026-08-24, comments)\n**Option 2 (remote-anchored horizon) + stale-clone forced override**, N=30d; residual loss of a >N-day-drifted clone's divergent work is ACCEPTED.\n\n## Design + proof (full detail in the DESIGN + PROOF comment)\nBoundary C1–C5: an event is reclaimable iff (C1) folded by a committed SNAPSHOT, (C2) that fold reachable on the sync remote's `tickets` ref ≥ N days, (C3) stale clones (>N days) forced to reset to remote, (C4) its signature is carried in the SNAPSHOT `authorship_ledger` OR `identity.enforce_since` is re-anchored to grandfather it, (C5) no unfolded state depends on it (I9). Under C1–C5: no still-observable state changes, no signature verification breaks, no resurrection.\n\nMeasured (origin/tickets): 235,482 commits, 408,295 trees, ZERO blobs blobless; trees ≈95% of bytes; root tree 270 KB × 6,008 flat ticket dirs re-emitted per commit; ~69% derived enrichment/digest churn; ~14K commits/day.\n\n## Quantified win — insufficient alone\n`.retired` files are blobs; the guarded pack is blobless ⇒ `.retired` GC shrinks it ~0. The lever is collapsing per-event history into SNAPSHOT-anchored checkpoints below the horizon. Max safe win N=30d ≈ 365–380 MiB; N=7d ≈ 200–215 MiB; ≤100 MiB needs an unsafe <4-day horizon. **A temporary budget-raise bridge (W3) is unavoidable**, paired with structural fixes (out of scope, `discovered_from` this epic): W1 batch the enrichment/digest write lane; W2 shard the flat ticket dirs to shrink root-tree churn.\n\n## Mechanism (portable, out-of-band; NO CI-provider dependence)\nOffline reclamation in a disposable clone (I9b isolation), never interactive, never under the store lock:\n- **S1** remote-anchored eligibility — pure git reachability + timestamps.\n- **S2** coalesce to a re-signed baseline checkpoint = compacted as-of-horizon state + carried authorship_ledger; re-anchor `enforce_since` to the baseline. Broadened from `.retired`-only to commit/tree reclamation.\n- **S3** stale-clone detection + forced remote-override on reconnect.\n- **S0** ADR: amend §I1 permanent-retention doctrine; prove I1–I9 under the horizon with the accepted >N-day loss.\n\n## Feasibility (resolves the surfaced questions)\n**Publish-swap = a per-backend adapter over ONE precondition (\"replace published history iff remote unchanged since T0, else abort+retry\"); no clone registry, no hold-open window (G6/T1/T3).** All clones auto-push to the ONE `sync.remote` (the single choke point). Adapter per backend: (a) git ref remotes (bare/GitHub/Gerrit) — `git push --force-with-lease=refs/heads/tickets:T0`, the same old→new ref CAS ADR 0031 uses. (b) S3 `git-remote-s3` backend (ADR 0093) has NO ref CAS — it stores bundle-per-push and its non-interactive auto-doctor (`s3_doctor.py`) folds divergent heads by lossless merge and NEVER discards a head, so a normally-pushed rewrite would be MERGED BACK (resurrected). So on S3 the reclaim is an EXCLUSIVE offline bundle-set replacement: under git-remote-s3's per-ref push-lock, enumerate bundles (`get_bundles_for_ref`), assert the head set == T0 (the CAS precondition), back up the bundle set to a rollback prefix, write ONE rewritten bundle and delete the originals — deliberately BYPASSING the auto-doctor. No acknowledged write is lost (a changed head set aborts+retries). Clients reconcile post-swap by EVENT-FILE REPLAY onto the baseline (append-only files, not history merge); >N-day offline clones lose divergent work (accepted).\n**Ledger reconciliation (G6):** the authorship_ledger keys on the stable `position` (`{ts}-{uuid}`), invariant across the rewrite; only the resolved `position.commit_sha` annotation and `enforce_since` (a ref) change. The same S2 pass re-resolves `position→commit_sha` in the NEW graph, rewrites the SNAPSHOT ledger annotations, and re-anchors `enforce_since` to the baseline — so `verify_authorship_at_commit`'s ancestry era-check evaluates consistently within one graph (in-horizon events verify against it; pre-baseline events grandfathered).\n**Rewrite primitive = core-git (T3):** emit new history with `git fast-import` (or `git mktree`/`commit-tree`) — ships with git, no `git filter-repo` (not vendored); preflight asserts `git fast-import --version`.\n\n## Risk / rollback\nA rewrite changes every commit SHA ⇒ every clone/worktree diverges (non-FF update of a shared auto-pushed branch); the CAS/exclusive-replace precondition bounds in-flight loss to the accepted >N-day case. Rollback: immutable backup ref `refs/tickets-backup/pre-reclaim-<date>` (or the S3 rollback bundle prefix) before the swap; stage on a shadow branch verified by `rebar fsck` + full-log replay + `verify-authorship`; the swap is one revertible step; never prune the backup until a green Verify Authorship Identity run + full reconcile.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Causal-stability boundary C1–C5 specified with citations.\n- [ ] C1–C5 proven to preserve still-observable ticket state (reads reduce from the SNAPSHOT).\n- [ ] C1–C5 proven to preserve signature/authorship verification.\n- [ ] C1–C5 proven to prevent resurrection across all clone states, incl. a <N-day clone with unpushed writes.\n- [ ] Publish-swap precondition realized as a git-ref CAS on git remotes.\n- [ ] Publish-swap realized as an exclusive push-locked bundle-set replacement (auto-doctor bypassed) on the S3 backend.\n- [ ] Ledger `position→commit_sha` re-resolution plus `enforce_since` re-anchor specified so authorship verifies on the rewritten graph.\n- [ ] History-rewrite primitive named as core-git and its portability resolved.\n- [ ] Pack-shrink quantified from measured object composition against the 100 MiB budget.\n- [ ] Coalescing shown insufficient alone at any safe horizon.\n- [ ] W3 budget-bridge value identified as the near-term unblock.\n- [ ] W1/W2 structural follow-ups identified as the durable fix.\n- [ ] Risk assessment plus rollback (backup ref, shadow-branch stage) designed.\n- [ ] Escalated to a human for approval before any destructive/history-rewriting action.\n\n## Deferred child-slice work (S0–S3 — NOT implemented here; only after approval)\n- [ ] S0 ADR: §I1 doctrine amended; I1–I9 proven under horizon plus override; accepted >N-day loss stated.\n- [ ] S1 eligibility algorithm implemented (remote-reachable ≥ N days; recent excluded; portable).\n- [ ] S2 coalescing prototyped on a DISPOSABLE SHADOW CLONE (never pushed); fsck/replay/verify-authorship clean on that shadow branch; two-clone resurrection test.\n- [ ] S3 stale-clone override enforced on reconnect.\n- [ ] docs/concurrency.md §I1 moved to 'implemented'; residual pack size quantified; W3 set; W1/W2 filed.\n\n## HARD STOP (this design slice only)\nDESIGN + PROOF only: no history rewrite / force-push / destructive reclaim executed; none of the deferred S0–S3 items done here. All S0–S3 implementation (incl. the S2 shadow-clone prototype) happens in later child slices AFTER human approval, and never mutates origin/tickets or the Gerrit box until the approved staged rollout. Escalated to the orchestrator for human approval before any destructive action."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788039580620681272,"uuid":"c881f7f8-3ad4-4106-9b29-b826d095f157"}